        instructions: 'Read the passage and answer all questions accurately.',
        contentText:
          '[A] Global internet infrastructure relies almost exclusively on an intricate planetary web of transoceanic submarine fiber-optic cables. Over ninety-nine percent of intercontinental telecommunications and financial transaction data travel along these hair-thin glass strands laid across the abyssal seafloor. However, the exponential expansion of cloud computing, artificial intelligence workloads, and streaming bandwidth demands is rapidly pushing conventional copper-based electronic interconnects and standard erbium-doped fiber amplifiers toward their thermodynamic Shannon capacity limits. In response, telecommunications engineers are deploying silicon photonics as a transformative technological paradigm.\n\n[B] Silicon photonics merges the exceptional data transmission capacity of optical laser light with the mature, low-cost fabrication ecosystem of complementary metal-oxide-semiconductor (CMOS) microchips. By etching microscopic optical waveguides directly onto standard silicon wafers, engineers can route optical signals with negligible attenuation and minimal electromagnetic crosstalk. Crucially, silicon is transparent to infrared wavelengths commonly utilized in optical communications, specifically the 1310-nanometer and 1550-nanometer transmission windows. Integrating micro-ring modulators and photodetectors onto a single monolithic die enables transceivers to process data rates exceeding 800 gigabits per second per wavelength.\n\n[C] A primary engineering bottleneck in transoceanic cables has historically centered on thermal dissipation and electrical power consumption in subterranean repeaters. Deep-sea repeaters, positioned at intervals of fifty to seventy kilometers along the ocean floor, must boost weakening optical signals using electrical power delivered from terrestrial shore stations via a single copper conductor. Conventional electronic multiplexers generate substantial resistive heat, constraining the total electrical current that can be safely transmitted over thousands of nautical miles. Silicon photonic transceivers operate with near-zero resistive energy losses, reducing repeater power budgets by up to forty percent.\n\n[D] To further multiply transmission capacity without laying additional physical cables, modern networks utilize space-division multiplexing (SDM) alongside multicore optical fibers. Rather than carrying signals down a single central silica glass core, SDM cables incorporate up to sixteen discrete optical cores within a single protective cladding. Coherent optical digital signal processors (DSPs) implemented via silicon photonics compensate dynamically for chromatic dispersion, polarization mode dispersion, and nonlinear fiber Kerr effects in real time. This technological synthesis allows modern transatlantic cables to achieve aggregate capacities exceeding 250 terabits per second.\n\n[E] Despite these remarkable theoretical and experimental advancements, commercial deployment of deep-sea silicon photonics encounters severe environmental challenges. Subsea cables must withstand extreme hydrostatic pressures, tectonic seismic shocks, and accidental snagging by commercial deep-sea fishing trawlers. Hermetically sealing delicate optoelectronic packages against high-pressure saline immersion requires specialized titanium alloy enclosures and glass-to-metal seals. Furthermore, repairing a severed subsea cable requires deploying specialized cable-laying vessels for weeks at immense monetary cost.\n\n[F] Looking toward the coming decade, researchers are investigating the integration of quantum key distribution (QKD) and photonic neural networks directly into subsea telecommunications nodes. Photonic tensor cores capable of performing mathematical matrix multiplications at the speed of light could process cryptographic handshakes without electrical conversion. As planetary digital interconnectivity deepens, silicon photonics will serve as the indispensable technological bedrock uniting terrestrial cloud data centers with deep-sea transoceanic arteries.',
