        title: 'Agricultural Resilience in Arid Climates: The Science of Halophyte Farming',
        instructions: 'Read the passage and answer all questions accurately.',
        contentText:
          '[A] Freshwater scarcity represents one of the most pressing agronomic crises of the twenty-first century. As subterranean aquifers become depleted and climate-induced droughts intensify across the Mediterranean, Middle East, and Sub-Saharan Africa, conventional irrigation with sweet water is increasingly unsustainable. Compounding this challenge, decades of intensive irrigation have precipitated secondary soil salinization, rendering millions of hectares of arable land toxic to conventional staple crops such as wheat, maize, and soybeans. In response, agricultural scientists are exploring biosaline agriculture, specifically the commercial cultivation of halophytes\u2014salt-tolerant plants that thrive in saline soils and can be irrigated with seawater.\n\n[B] Halophytes represent a specialized evolutionary group comprising approximately two percent of terrestrial angiosperm plant species. Unlike glycophytes (salt-sensitive plants), which suffer cellular dehydration and cytotoxic sodium accumulation when exposed to high salt concentrations, halophytes possess specialized anatomical and biochemical adaptations. True halophytes, such as Salicornia europaea (glasswort) and Atriplex canescens (saltbush), actively accumulate inorganic ions within their vacuolar compartments to maintain favorable osmotic water potential. By compartmentalizing toxic sodium and chloride ions away from sensitive cytoplasmic enzymes, these plants sustain photosynthesis and cell division in hyper-saline environments.\n\n[C] Beyond vacuolar compartmentalization, halophytes deploy external salt excretion structures. Species such as Avicennia marina and Tamarix articulata possess microscopic epidermal salt glands and bladder hairs on their foliage. These specialized glandular organs actively secrete excess sodium and chloride onto the leaf surface, where salt crystals crystallize and are shed by wind or rainfall. Concurrently, halophytic root membranes exhibit exceptional selectivity, absorbing vital potassium and calcium ions while excluding excessive sodium influx at the Casparian strip.\n\n[D] The agronomic potential of halophytic species extends far beyond theoretical botanical interest. Salicornia, an edible succulent rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids, vitamin C, and dietary fiber, is cultivated as a gourmet vegetable in European and Gulf agricultural markets. Furthermore, its oil-rich seeds contain up to thirty percent high-grade vegetable oil suitable for both culinary consumption and biofuel synthesis. Agricultural trials in coastal desert regions demonstrate that irrigated Salicornia fields can produce seed oil yields comparable to conventional terrestrial oilseed crops while relying exclusively on raw ocean seawater.\n\n[E] Halophyte biomass also provides nutritious livestock forage for sheep, goats, and camels in arid coastal margins. Atriplex shrubs synthesize high concentrations of crude protein, mitigating the need for imported grain feedstuffs. When integrated into silvopastoral agroforestry models, perennial halophyte hedgerows stabilize desert sand dunes, arrest soil erosion, and sequester substantial quantities of atmospheric carbon in deep root systems. However, livestock diets incorporating high-salt forage require careful nutritional balancing to prevent renal electrolyte stress and excessive water consumption.\n\n[F] Despite these compelling agronomic and environmental advantages, widespread commercial adoption of halophyte farming faces economic and infrastructural hurdles. Seawater irrigation demands durable, corrosion-resistant pumping machinery and advanced subsurface drainage networks to prevent the irreversible accumulation of salt crusts on underlying soil horizons. Moreover, establishing commercial consumer markets for unfamiliar halophyte products requires sustained marketing and food processing innovation. Nevertheless, as freshwater reserves dwindle, halophyte biosaline farming offers a scientifically proven pathway to sustainable desert greening and food security.',
