        title: 'Neurological Plasticity and Second Language Acquisition in Adults',
        instructions: 'Read the passage and answer all questions accurately.',
        contentText:
          "[A] For over half a century, the Critical Period Hypothesis dominated second language acquisition research. Formulated by neurobiologist Eric Lenneberg in 1967, this theory posited that successful language learning is biologically bounded by childhood brain lateralization, which terminates around puberty. After this developmental milestone, the human brain supposedly loses the neural plasticity required to attain native-like phonetic pronunciation and syntactic intuition. Consequently, adult language learners were long viewed as cognitive underdogs, condemned to speak with persistent foreign accents and struggle with grammatical irregularities.\n\n[B] However, recent breakthroughs in functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) and magnetoencephalography (MEG) have thoroughly debunked this rigid biological determinism. Contemporary neuroscientists have demonstrated that the adult brain retains remarkable neuroplasticity throughout the entire human lifespan. While young children absorb phonemes effortlessly through implicit statistical learning in subcortical structures, adult learners recruit sophisticated declarative memory systems in the prefrontal cortex. This mature executive capacity allows adults to grasp abstract grammatical frameworks and semantic collocations far more rapidly than young infants.\n\n[C] Structural neuroimaging studies reveal observable physical changes in the cerebral architecture of bilingual adults. Intensive second language study stimulates significant increases in the gray matter density of the left inferior parietal cortex\u2014a region critically involved in phonological processing and verbal working memory. Concurrently, bilingualism enhances the structural integrity of white matter tracts, notably the superior longitudinal fasciculus, which connects Broca's expressive language area with Wernicke's receptive comprehension zone. These reinforced neural pathways facilitate rapid linguistic code-switching.\n\n[D] Beyond linguistic proficiency, acquiring an additional language confers broad cognitive benefits that shield the aging brain against neurodegenerative decline. Bilingual individuals routinely outperform monolingual peers on non-verbal executive function tasks requiring inhibitory control, cognitive flexibility, and attentional switching. By continually suppressing their native tongue while activating second language vocabulary, bilinguals build substantial cognitive reserve. Epidemiological studies demonstrate that active bilingualism delays the onset of Alzheimer's clinical symptoms by an average of four to five years.\n\n[E] Crucially, pedagogical methodology exerts a profound impact on adult neural reorganization. Traditional grammar-translation instruction, which emphasizes rote vocabulary memorization and isolated rules, fails to engage subcortical procedural memory circuits. In contrast, communicative immersion and spaced-repetition retrieval practice activate dopamine-mediated synaptic consolidation pathways. When adult learners practice active spoken recall in interactive contexts, newly formed synaptic connections are stabilized through long-term potentiation (LTP).\n\n[F] In conclusion, adult second language acquisition is not constrained by an insurmountable biological deadline, but by the efficiency of cognitive engagement. The adult human brain remains dynamically plastic, capable of re-wiring its neural circuits to achieve exceptional linguistic fluency. Embracing scientifically grounded cognitive strategies empowers adult scholars to master complex linguistic repertoires while bolstering long-term cerebral resilience.",
